{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 756150,
  "courseSlug": "the-complete-guide-to-angular-2",
  "courseTitle": "Angular - The Complete Guide",
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "43788588",
      "title": "Accepting Objects As Inputs & Adding Appropriate Typings",
      "lectureOrder": 40,
      "videoOrder": 36,
      "sourceHash": "a0787e88d6528f468e2facf02d5877d17ac39e6ed5d85125ecca829faa8f0d5a",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 0.66,
          "end": 2.55,
          "en": "Now when working with TypeScript,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 2.55,
          "end": 7.38,
          "en": "it's not just about managing possibly undefined values,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 7.38,
          "end": 9.33,
          "en": "though that is one situation",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 9.33,
          "end": 11.82,
          "en": "where TypeScript can indeed shine.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 11.82,
          "end": 15.66,
          "en": "But it's also pretty common when working with TypeScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 15.66,
          "end": 19.14,
          "en": "that you need to define in advance which type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 19.14,
          "end": 22.41,
          "en": "of value might be stored somewhere like here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 22.41,
          "end": 25.65,
          "en": "where we're saying that it's a string or undefined",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 25.65,
          "end": 27.54,
          "en": "because of this question mark.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 27.54,
          "end": 31.812,
          "en": "And you can see the actual type that is in the end assumed",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 31.812,
          "end": 34.5,
          "en": "for name by hovering over it and you see that it's a string",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 34.5,
          "end": 35.943,
          "en": "or undefined",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 38,
          "end": 40.86,
          "en": "or as we do it in the app component",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 40.86,
          "end": 44.01,
          "en": "where we have this method where we have to tell TypeScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 44.01,
          "end": 47.94,
          "en": "that the ID we receive here will be a string",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 47.94,
          "end": 51.483,
          "en": "and just a string, not undefined or anything like that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 52.62,
          "end": 57.09,
          "en": "It's pretty common that you must define types like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 57.09,
          "end": 60.78,
          "en": "And thus far we pretty much only worked with strings,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 60.78,
          "end": 62.46,
          "en": "which is one of the most basic",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 62.46,
          "end": 65.16,
          "en": "and also common types you'll deal with",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 65.16,
          "end": 66.993,
          "en": "when working with TypeScript.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 68.07,
          "end": 69.6,
          "en": "But we could also work",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 69.6,
          "end": 72.72,
          "en": "with more complex types if we wanted to.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 72.72,
          "end": 76.23,
          "en": "Like for example here in the user component.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 76.23,
          "end": 81.09,
          "en": "Here, we have three inputs where we get the user's ID,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 81.09,
          "end": 82.68299999999999,
          "en": "avatar and name.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 83.61,
          "end": 87.78,
          "en": "And of course if you take a look at the dummy user data,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 87.78,
          "end": 92.36,
          "en": "you can see that users are in the end only made up",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 92.36,
          "end": 95.49000000000001,
          "en": "of these three pieces of data.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 95.49000000000001,
          "end": 96.32300000000001,
          "en": "So in the end,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 96.32300000000001,
          "end": 100.11,
          "en": "we could also simplify the code in the user component",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 100.11,
          "end": 103.89,
          "en": "by instead of accepting three properties here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 103.89,
          "end": 108.303,
          "en": "just accepting one property, the entire user object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 109.56,
          "end": 112.35,
          "en": "So we could remove this code",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 112.35,
          "end": 114.959,
          "en": "and instead add a single input property,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 114.959,
          "end": 116.913,
          "en": "which could be called user.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 117.99000000000001,
          "end": 120.603,
          "en": "And this should then again, be required.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 122.22,
          "end": 124.8,
          "en": "But now we also have to set the type for that,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 124.8,
          "end": 126.913,
          "en": "and that's now not just a string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 126.913,
          "end": 131.49,
          "en": "Instead it's now an object with multiple properties",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 131.49,
          "end": 133.53,
          "en": "where all those properties",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 133.53,
          "end": 136.833,
          "en": "then also have different types of values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 138,
          "end": 140.37,
          "en": "But that is something you can do in TypeScript.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 140.37,
          "end": 145.17000000000002,
          "en": "You can create a so-called object type by using curly braces",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 145.17000000000002,
          "end": 146.57999999999998,
          "en": "just as you would do it",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 146.57999999999998,
          "end": 149.37,
          "en": "if you would create an object literal.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 149.37,
          "end": 152.25,
          "en": "However, here we're not creating a new object,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 152.25,
          "end": 155.7,
          "en": "but instead we are creating a type here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 155.7,
          "end": 157.8,
          "en": "And that's an important difference.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 157.8,
          "end": 160.62,
          "en": "We're just telling TypeScript which kind",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 160.62,
          "end": 163.92000000000002,
          "en": "of value will eventually get here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 163.92000000000002,
          "end": 167.91,
          "en": "And now you can set up all the properties that you expect",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 167.91,
          "end": 171.18,
          "en": "to be in that object type,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 171.18,
          "end": 173.1,
          "en": "for example, an ID.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 173.1,
          "end": 176.25,
          "en": "But now you also have to tell TypeScript which kind",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 176.25,
          "end": 179.67000000000002,
          "en": "of value will be stored in that property.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 179.67000000000002,
          "end": 181.8,
          "en": "And you do that again by adding a colon here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 181.8,
          "end": 184.35,
          "en": "and then the type, so not a value,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 184.35,
          "end": 186.633,
          "en": "but the type in this case, a string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 187.68,
          "end": 189.3,
          "en": "Then you can add a semicolon",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 189.3,
          "end": 192.3,
          "en": "and define the next property type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 192.3,
          "end": 194.91,
          "en": "So for example, that we will have an avatar,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 194.91,
          "end": 196.59,
          "en": "which will also be a string",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 196.59,
          "end": 199.113,
          "en": "and a name, which will also be a string,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 200.16,
          "end": 203.553,
          "en": "and that would now be our user object type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 204.75,
          "end": 206.25,
          "en": "Now of course we still have",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 206.25,
          "end": 208.92000000000002,
          "en": "to add this exclamation mark in addition",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 208.92000000000002,
          "end": 210.18,
          "en": "to setting up this type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 210.18,
          "end": 213.06,
          "en": "because we still need to convince TypeScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 213.06,
          "end": 215.25,
          "en": "that this will definitely be defined",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 215.25,
          "end": 217.953,
          "en": "because of this required true setting.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 218.85,
          "end": 221.1,
          "en": "Now the code of course, must be adjusted.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 221.1,
          "end": 224.4,
          "en": "For example, here, instead of accessing this avatar,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 224.4,
          "end": 227.16,
          "en": "it's this.user.avatar.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 227.16,
          "end": 230.073,
          "en": "And down here it's this.user.id.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 231.36,
          "end": 235.71,
          "en": "And in the template of the user component HTML file,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 235.71,
          "end": 240.543,
          "en": "we have to access user.name and also user.name here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 242.4,
          "end": 246.54,
          "en": "And we now also must adjust the app component template",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 246.54,
          "end": 248.4,
          "en": "because instead of now passing",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 248.4,
          "end": 252.03,
          "en": "all these individual properties to app user,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 252.03,
          "end": 255.06,
          "en": "we now just pass the user property",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 255.06,
          "end": 258.84,
          "en": "and bind it dynamically to users.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 258.84,
          "end": 262.59,
          "en": "Zero here for the first app user",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 262.59,
          "end": 266.64300000000003,
          "en": "to users one for the second app user.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 269.13,
          "end": 272.4,
          "en": "And of course then to users two",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 272.4,
          "end": 277.32,
          "en": "and to users three down here, and now if I format this,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 277.32,
          "end": 279.603,
          "en": "this all is a bit more readable again.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 280.95,
          "end": 284.133,
          "en": "But that's how you can set up such a object type.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
